        <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">One car. One condition grade. Eight quotes that do not agree with each other.</p>

        <p>That is the entire premise of a used car price city comparison, and it is the part most owners discover late, usually after a quote from a buyer three states away lands higher than the one from the dealer down the road. The resale value city difference in India is not noise. It is structural, it repeats month after month, and it traces back to four identifiable causes. Before reading further, it is worth putting the actual car through a <a href="/valuation">free used car valuation</a> so the numbers below have something to sit against.</p>

        <p>A caution up front, because it changes how the rest of this should be read. The city with the highest retail premium is not automatically the city that pays a seller the most.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Why used car resale value differs by city in India: four levers, not one</h2>

        <p>Used car resale value differs across Indian cities because the same car carries different amounts of usable legal life, faces a different fuel-type buyer mix, arrives at inspection with a different wear signature, and costs a different amount to move out of state. Those four levers stack. A car can be penalised on all four at once, which is how a five-figure gap becomes a six-figure one.</p>

        <p>The order matters, and this article follows it:</p>

        <ol class="list-decimal ml-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Remaining legal life of the registration.</strong> How many years the car is still permitted to ply where it is registered.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Fuel-type buyer mix.</strong> Petrol, diesel or CNG, and how strongly local buyers want it.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">City wear signature.</strong> What the roads, traffic and climate have already done to the car's mechanicals and body.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Transfer friction.</strong> The cost, in money and elapsed weeks, of taking the car somewhere else.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>City is one input among many. Age, kilometres, variant and service history still do the heavy lifting, and <a href="/blogs/factors-affecting-used-car-resale-value">the 10 factors that affect used car resale value</a> covers how they rank against each other.</p>

        <p>Here is the thesis, stated plainly so it is not mistaken for a "sell in Bengaluru" listicle. In the highest-premium metros, a larger share of the retail spread gets absorbed by dealer margin and pre-sale reconditioning than in the discount metros. The retail gap between two cities is almost always wider than the seller-payout gap between the same two cities. Anyone planning around the headline number is planning around the wrong number.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Used car price city comparison: how the same benchmark car prices across eight metros</h2>

        <p>A generic city table proves nothing. So the comparison below is fixed to a single car, a single variant and a single kilometre band, then read across eight registration markets. Benchmark A: a 2019 Maruti Suzuki Baleno Zeta petrol manual at roughly 45,000 km, single owner, no accident history, full service record.</p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b-2 border-navy-900 text-navy-900 text-left">
                <th class="py-2 pr-3">Registration market</th>
                <th class="py-2 pr-3">Position vs national median</th>
                <th class="py-2">Dominant demand driver</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-graphite">
              <tr class="border-b border-cream-200"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Delhi-NCR</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">Discount</td><td class="py-2">Shortened legal life on petrol (15 yrs) compresses residuals</td></tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-cream-200"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Mumbai (MMR)</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">Premium</td><td class="py-2">Short daily running favours compact petrol</td></tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-cream-200"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Bengaluru</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">Highest premium</td><td class="py-2">Salaried first-time buyers, deep 3 to 7 year hatch demand</td></tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-cream-200"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Chennai</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">Near median</td><td class="py-2">Service-history-led buyers; corrosion checks bite</td></tr>
