              <tr class="border-b border-cream-200"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Pune</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">Near median</td><td class="py-2">Two-way inventory flow with MMR caps the spread</td></tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-cream-200"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Kolkata</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">Softest</td><td class="py-2">Price-sensitive mix, older average fleet</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="py-2 pr-3">Ahmedabad</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">Discount</td><td class="py-2">Value-led buyers, strong CNG tilt</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>The petrol-only benchmark hides half the picture. The same eight-market read across a 2018 Hyundai Creta SX diesel at 70,000 km inverts several positions, because the petrol and diesel lines move in opposite directions in Delhi-NCR. That is why a single-fuel table misrepresents the country badly.</p>

        <p class="text-sm text-slate-soft"><strong class="text-navy-900">Method note.</strong> "Premium" here means the difference between a city's price for this exact car and the national median for the same car in the same month. It is a comparison across markets, not a forecast for any individual car. Two cars of the same model and year can quote differently in the same city on condition alone.</p>

        <p>The buy column at a dealer desk and the retail column at the same dealer's front lot are not the same number, and the difference is not arbitrary. It covers reconditioning, holding cost, warranty provisioning and margin. That gap gets unpacked separately in a comparison of fair-market value, asking price and dealer offer, when that page publishes.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Delhi used car resale value: how the 10-year diesel and 15-year petrol rule prices a car by its birthdate</h2>

        <p>In Delhi-NCR, a car is not priced primarily by condition. It is priced by how many months of permitted registration life it has left. That single fact explains why used cars are cheaper in Delhi than in most other metros, and why the Delhi depreciation curve behaves like a cliff rather than a slope.</p>

        <p>The rule, in plain language: within the National Capital Region, diesel vehicles older than 10 years and petrol vehicles older than 15 years are not permitted to ply on the roads. The restriction originates in National Green Tribunal directions and has been carried forward through Commission for Air Quality Management orders and Delhi Transport Department de-registration notices. Enforcement has been announced, deferred and revised more than once. Anyone acting on the current position should verify the latest directive before quoting a specific car.</p>

        <p>The ripple runs across the whole NCR. Gurugram, Noida, Ghaziabad and Faridabad registrations sit under the same regime. Re-registering the car within NCR does not reset the clock.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">The 8-year diesel wall: what happens to a Delhi diesel's price in its ninth year</h3>

        <p>A Delhi buyer inherits only the remaining months, not the car's mechanical life. So the discount applied has nothing to do with how well the engine runs.</p>

        <p>Take the Creta SX diesel benchmark at three ages in Delhi-NCR. At six years old, it prices close to a national-median diesel of the same age. At eight years, the discount widens noticeably because a buyer is now purchasing roughly two usable years. At nine, local demand thins to the point where quotes stop reflecting resale logic altogether and start reflecting relocation or scrappage logic. Owners in this band are often comparing a local offer against a <a href="/blogs/car-scrappage-value-india">scrappage value output</a> rather than against another resale offer.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">The NOC exit route: why a Delhi diesel is worth more outside Delhi than inside it</h3>

        <p>A diesel that has run out of permitted life in NCR may still hold value in a state that allows it to be re-registered, provided the origin RTO issues a valid No Objection Certificate, a written clearance confirming the registering authority has no objection to the car being registered elsewhere.</p>

        <p>Conditional, not automatic. That is the key detail. Several states have restricted or refused re-registration of vehicles that have reached end-of-life status in NCR, and the position has changed over time. Anyone planning a cross-state exit should confirm the destination state's current acceptance position before spending on transport.</p>

        <p>Who buys these cars? Typically dealers operating outside NCR who consolidate stock, and buyers in markets where an older diesel still has a functioning second life. The price they pay is set by the destination market's rules, minus transport and re-registration cost.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Car resale value in Bengaluru: the highest premium in India, and the suspension bill that eats part of it</h2>

        <p>Bengaluru consistently carries the strongest demand-side pull among Indian metros for mainstream used cars, and it also produces the country's most consistent city-specific mechanical defect pattern. Both halves are real. Quoting only the first half gives sellers a number they will not receive.</p>

        <p>Demand first. Bengaluru's used-car buyer base skews heavily toward salaried first-time buyers and short-tenure residents who need a car for a defined period. That sustains unusually high absorption of 3 to 7 year-old hatchbacks and compact SUVs. Baleno, Swift, Nexon, i20 and the compact SUV set move quickly and hold price.</p>

        <p>Then there is the gearbox. The automatic transmission premium is wider in Bengaluru than in any other Indian metro, and it is not close. Commute density does that. The same AMT or torque-converter variant of a model commands a distinctly higher spread over its manual sibling in Bengaluru than the identical pair does in Ahmedabad.</p>

        <p>No Karnataka age cap of the NCR type applies, which is why 12 and 14-year-old petrol cars still trade in Bengaluru in a market that has effectively closed in Delhi.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">What a full inspection finds on a Bengaluru car that it does not find on an Ahmedabad car</h3>

        <p>The city's road surface and traffic pattern show up in the inspection bay before they show up in the offer. Front lower-arm bushes, suspension links and strut mounts wear earlier on Bengaluru-registered cars than they do on the same model registered elsewhere. Alloy rim kerb and pothole damage is more frequent. Clutch or AMT wear from stop-go running turns up at lower kilometre readings. Steering rack play appears at above-average rates on the older arterial routes.</p>

        <p>The commercial consequence is straightforward. A car that arrives at a Bengaluru retail lot needing suspension work absorbs part of the city premium before it reaches the display floor. So the seller-side gap between Bengaluru and a median city is narrower than the retail-side gap in the table above. Sellers weighing the routes should look at the private, dealer and instant-buy comparison, when that page publishes, before assuming the premium reaches them.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Used car price Delhi vs Mumbai: same model, opposite fuel logic</h2>

        <p>For the same benchmark car, Mumbai and Delhi diverge because their buyers run different distances on different fuels in different body types. Mumbai rewards small petrol and automatic cars with short daily running. Delhi rewards large SUVs and premium sedans with long running, while simultaneously discounting every car for shortened legal life. The same Baleno and the same Creta land on opposite sides of that logic.</p>

        <p>Distance and space dictate Mumbai's structure. Average daily running in MMR is short, traffic is dense, and parking is genuinely constrained. Diesel's running-cost advantage only pays back over distance, so where distances are shortest, the diesel premium is structurally thin. Compact petrol and automatic variants pick up the demand instead.</p>

        <p>Most people treat parking as a lifestyle irritation rather than a pricing variable. That is only half the story. In parts of Mumbai, society parking allocation and physical bay length narrow the buyer pool for longer SUVs and full-size sedans, and a car that cannot fit a stilt bay loses a slice of its local audience.</p>

        <p>Delhi's counter-position is a deeper large-SUV and premium-sedan market, longer average running that keeps diesel demand alive right up to the age limit, and stronger commercial and fleet resale flow. All of it sitting on a shorter clock.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">The monsoon file: what a Mumbai inspection checks that a Delhi inspection does not</h3>

        <p>Water-damage checks on a Mumbai-registered car are line items that a Delhi inspection barely touches. Boot-well and spare-wheel cavity staining. Sill and underbody corrosion. Seat-rail rust. Wiring-harness and ECU moisture markers. Mismatched or replaced carpet. Silt residue in the cabin blower housing. A car flagged for flood exposure is often quoted at a steep discount rather than declined outright, but the discount is wide enough that some sellers prefer to disclose in writing and let the buyer walk away from a low offer at the kerb rather than accept a re-negotiation on the day of handover.</p>

